update public.content_post set title_en = $d175$Batch Brew Basics: Ratio, Grind and Holding Time for Cafe Filter Coffee$d175$, excerpt_en = $d175$Batch brew comes down to three rules. Start at around 55g of coffee per litre of water, dial in flavour with grind, and brew only what you can sell within an hour.$d175$, body_html_en = $d175$<p>If your cafe gets a rush of filter coffee orders in the morning, batch brew may be the answer. Batch brew means brewing several cups at once on a coffee machine, holding the coffee in an insulated container and serving from it. The short answer comes down to three rules: start at around 55g of coffee per litre of water, dial in flavour with grind size, and brew only as much as you can sell within an hour.</p>
<h2>Terms and definitions</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Batch brew</td><td>Filter coffee brewed several cups at a time on a machine</td></tr><tr><td>Brew ratio</td><td>The ratio of coffee weight to water weight</td></tr><tr><td>Holding time</td><td>How long brewed coffee is kept in the container</td></tr><tr><td>TDS</td><td>The concentration of dissolved solids in the coffee</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>Ratio: start at 55g per litre of water</h2>
<p>The Specialty Coffee Association (SCA) uses 55g of coffee per 1,000g of water as the reference ratio in its coffee brewer standard. That is roughly 1:18. Converted for cafe use, it looks like this.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Water per batch</th><th>Coffee (1:18)</th><th>Coffee (1:17)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1L</td><td>55g</td><td>59g</td></tr><tr><td>2L</td><td>110g</td><td>118g</td></tr><tr><td>3L</td><td>165g</td><td>176g</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>1:18 is a starting point. If your guests like a stronger cup, add coffee and move toward 1:17 or 1:16. For reference, the pour over recipe for MTSPACE Allrounder is 12g to 192g, which is 1:16.</p>
<h2>Dial in flavour with grind size</h2>
<p>Once the ratio is set, adjust flavour with grind. Every machine keeps water in contact with the coffee for a different length of time, so the grind you use for pour over may not fit. Check brew time and taste on your first batch. Then move one step at a time as below.</p>
<ul><li>Sour and thin: a little finer</li><li>Bitter and astringent: a little coarser</li><li>Only lacking strength: keep the grind and add more coffee</li></ul>
<h2>Holding time: brew only what sells within an hour</h2>
<p>Brewed coffee loses aroma and changes in taste as time passes. The SCA coffee brewer standard requires the holding container to keep coffee at 80 to 85℃ for the first 30 minutes, and many cafes use selling within one hour as their working rule. An insulated airpot keeps flavour longer than a glass server sitting on a hot plate.</p>
<p>So keep batches modest. Brew larger batches more often during the morning peak, switch to smaller batches in the afternoon, and write the brew time on the container.</p>
<h2>An example routine</h2>
<ol><li>Brew a 2L batch right after opening and write the time on the container.</li><li>After an hour, check how much is left.</li><li>If a lot is left, cut the next batch to 1L.</li><li>After a week of notes, you will see the right batch size for each time of day.</li></ol>
<h2>Which coffee suits batch brew</h2>
<p>Because you brew many cups at once, a coffee whose taste does not swing much from day to day is easier. Consistency is the strength of a blend, while a single origin shows the character of its origin and is easy to rotate based on guest response. For your first batch brew, we recommend starting with one blend. You can see how our blends differ in <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/blogs/coffeelog/blog-blend-4-comparison">our four blends compared</a>.</p>
<h2>Want to use these coffees in your venue</h2>
<p>For batch brew coffee and recipe advice, send us a <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/contact?type=wholesale">wholesale enquiry</a>. Wholesale pricing is applied to your account as soon as your business is verified after <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/account/signup?role=business">business registration</a>.</p>
<h2>FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q. Can I simply scale up my pour over recipe?</strong><br />You can keep the ratio. But a machine holds water on the coffee for a different time, so you will need to dial in the grind again.</p>
<p><strong>Q. Can I sell leftover coffee as iced coffee?</strong><br />Coffee that has been held for a while has already changed in aroma. Brewing your iced menu separately protects the taste.</p>
<p><strong>Q. What holding container should I use?</strong><br />An insulated airpot is better than a hot plate. Preheat it with hot water before use so the temperature drops less.</p>
<h3>Sources</h3>
<ul><li>Reference ratio (55g of coffee per 1,000g of water) and holding temperature (80 to 85℃ for the first 30 minutes): <a href="https://sca.coffee/s/SCA_Standard_310-2021.pdf">SCA Standard 310-2021</a></li><li>Holding time practice (sell within one hour): <a href="https://blackoakcoffee.com/blogs/black-oak-wholesale-blog/the-business-of-coffee-how-to-design-an-effective-batch-brew-system">Black Oak Coffee, How to design an effective batch brew system</a></li><li>Allrounder reference recipe: MTSPACE COFFEE product data</li></ul>$d175$, seo_title_en = $d175$Batch Brew Basics: 55g/L Ratio, Grind and Holding Time$d175$, seo_description_en = $d175$Cafe batch brew standards: start at 55g of coffee per litre, dial in with grind, and brew only what sells within an hour. Batch size table and a cafe routine.$d175$ where slug = 'blog-batch-brew-basics' and title_en is null and body_html_en is null;
update public.content_post set title_en = $d175$From Competition Recipe to Cafe Recipe: Move the Principles, Not the Numbers$d175$, excerpt_en = $d175$What you take from a competition recipe is the principle, not the numbers. How competitions and cafes differ, three principles to borrow, and five steps to a cafe recipe.$d175$, body_html_en = $d175$<p>Competition recipes look impressive, but most of them fail when moved straight into a cafe. A competition is a stage for making a few cups at their peak in a set time, while a cafe is a floor where many cups have to taste alike all day. The short answer: what you take from a competition recipe is the principle, not the numbers, and a cafe recipe has to be redesigned around repeatability.</p>
<h2>Competitions and cafes have different goals</h2>
<p>In the World Brewers Cup (WBrC) open service, competitors serve three cups of the same drink within ten minutes, and the head judge scores the consistency of those three cups separately. In the World Barista Championship (WBC), competitors serve twelve drinks in fifteen minutes. Both assume a short time, few cups and ideal conditions.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Competition</th><th>Cafe</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cups</td><td>Three to twelve</td><td>Tens to hundreds a day</td></tr><tr><td>Time</td><td>10 or 15 minutes</td><td>All opening hours</td></tr><tr><td>Who brews</td><td>One competitor</td><td>Several staff</td></tr><tr><td>Water</td><td>Designed water</td><td>The cafe's filtered water</td></tr><tr><td>Coffee</td><td>A small competition lot</td><td>A coffee with steady supply</td></tr><tr><td>Goal</td><td>One high score</td><td>A similar taste every time</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>Three principles to take from competition recipes</h2>
<p><strong>One: write the taste goal as a sentence first.</strong> A competition routine sets what the coffee should show in one sentence, then fits the recipe to it. If a cafe sets a goal first, such as "a cup where sweetness comes before acidity", the direction of recipe changes stays steady.</p>
<p><strong>Two: fix every variable as a number.</strong> Competition preparation measures ratio, grind, temperature and time so that three cups come out the same. A cafe recipe card also needs every item written as a number, so the same cup comes out when staff change.</p>
<p><strong>Three: change one thing at a time and check by taste.</strong> Competitors change a single variable on the same coffee and recipe and compare blind. In a cafe too, if you change the grind, keep everything else the same and taste.</p>
<h2>Turning it into a cafe recipe</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Re-taste with your cafe water.</strong> Designed competition water and the cafe's filtered water differ. The same recipe tastes different when the water changes.</li><li><strong>Widen the tolerances.</strong> Rather than matching to the second as in competition, set ranges such as "2:10 to 2:30" that staff can keep during busy hours.</li><li><strong>Simplify the movements.</strong> Complicated pour counts and patterns cause variation between staff. Drop movements that do not change the result much.</li><li><strong>Build the recipe on a coffee with steady supply.</strong> Use a coffee you can keep buying instead of a small competition lot, so the card stays useful for longer.</li><li><strong>Make it a card and check at every opening.</strong> A routine of measuring the first cup and noting its taste is what keeps a recipe.</li></ol>
<h2>How MTSPACE does it</h2>
<p>Founder Chanho Hong is a barista competitor with 14 podium finishes in Korea and Australia. MTSPACE applies the same light roast profile used in competition to its single origins, and publishes a reference recipe for each coffee that a cafe can start from straight away. For example, Ethiopia Kochere Danicho Washed is a washed coffee from Kochere Hama in Yirgacheffe, roasted on a light profile with first crack at 6 minutes 30 seconds, a 5℃ rise after crack and 45 seconds of development, and its cafe reference recipe is 15g for filter, 225g total, 2:10 to 2:30. We work on translating competition thinking into cafe numbers together through <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/consulting">consulting and barista training</a>. Competition structure and preparation are covered in our education chapters <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/en/education/wbrc-brewers-cup">WBrC World Brewers Cup</a> and <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/en/education/wbc-barista-championship">WBC World Barista Championship</a>.</p>
<h2>Want to use these coffees in your venue</h2>
<p>If you would like to receive coffee together with its reference recipe, please send a <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/contact?type=wholesale">wholesale enquiry</a>. Wholesale pricing is applied to your account as soon as your business is verified after <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/account/signup?role=business">business registration</a>.</p>
<h2>FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q. Can I not just use a competition recipe as it is?</strong><br />You can, but when water, people and coffee change, the same taste will not come out. Taking only the principles and re-fitting them to cafe conditions is more stable.</p>
<p><strong>Q. Taste differs between staff. What do I fix first?</strong><br />Start with a recipe card written in numbers and an opening check. If the movements are complicated, simplify them.</p>
<p><strong>Q. Does competition preparation help a cafe?</strong><br />The measuring, sensory and consistency habits learned in competition preparation carry straight over to cafe quality control.</p>
<h3>Sources</h3>
<ul><li>WBrC and WBC round structure and scoring: MTSPACE COFFEE education resource The Basic of Coffee, chapters 12 and 13 (based on the official 2025 WBrC and 2026 WBC rules)</li><li>Founder's record, roast profile and reference recipe: MTSPACE COFFEE records and product data</li></ul>$d175$, seo_title_en = $d175$Turning a Competition Recipe Into a Cafe Recipe$d175$, seo_description_en = $d175$Why WBC and WBrC recipes drift when used in a cafe, three principles worth borrowing, and five steps to a repeatable cafe recipe, from a barista competitor.$d175$ where slug = 'blog-competition-to-cafe-recipe' and title_en is null and body_html_en is null;
update public.content_post set title_en = $d175$Ethiopian Washed Recipe Card: Filter, Espresso and Milk Starting Points and Adjustments$d175$, excerpt_en = $d175$The filter, espresso and milk reference recipes for Ethiopia Kochere Danicho Washed on a single card, and what to change first when the taste is off.$d175$, body_html_en = $d175$<p>If you bought an Ethiopian washed coffee and the acidity is only sharp or the aroma feels blurry, the recipe starting point usually does not fit the coffee. Using MTSPACE Ethiopia Kochere Danicho Washed as the reference, this post puts the filter, espresso and milk recipes on a single card and shows what to change first when the taste is off.</p>
<h2>Reference recipe card</h2>
<p>The numbers below are the reference recipe for <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/products/ethiopia-kochere-danicho">Ethiopia Kochere Danicho Washed</a>. At home or in a cafe, start here and adjust for your equipment.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Brew</th><th>Coffee</th><th>Water or yield</th><th>Time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Filter (V60)</td><td>15g, grind 700</td><td>45g bloom for 90 seconds, 225g total</td><td>2:10 to 2:30</td></tr><tr><td>Espresso</td><td>18g</td><td>43g</td><td>24 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>Milk</td><td>20g</td><td>40g, 150ml milk</td><td>26 seconds</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>The filter ratio is 15g to 225g, or 1:15. The espresso pulls 43g from 18g, a ratio of about 1:2.4, slightly longer than the common 1:2.</p>
<h2>Filter brewing steps</h2>
<ol><li>Grind 15g of coffee at setting 700 and rinse the paper filter with hot water.</li><li>Pour 45g of water evenly to wet the grounds and wait 90 seconds.</li><li>Pour the remaining water in stages to reach 225g in total.</li><li>If the water has fully drained within 2:10 to 2:30, you are in the reference range.</li></ol>
<p>The 90 second bloom is the reference for this coffee. Check your grind before shortening it.</p>
<h2>Adjustment table when the taste is off</h2>
<p>Change only one thing at a time. If you change two at once, you cannot tell which one made the difference.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>If it tastes like this</th><th>Change first</th><th>Direction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sharp acidity with a short finish</td><td>Grind</td><td>A little finer</td></tr><tr><td>Lingering bitterness and astringency</td><td>Grind</td><td>A little coarser</td></tr><tr><td>Thin and watery</td><td>Dose</td><td>Add 1g</td></tr><tr><td>Brew time over 2:30</td><td>Grind</td><td>A little coarser</td></tr><tr><td>Brew time under 2:10</td><td>Grind</td><td>A little finer</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>The same principle applies to espresso. If the shot finishes faster than 24 seconds, grind finer; if it runs slower, grind coarser.</p>
<h2>The taste changes with temperature</h2>
<p>Kochere Danicho's cup notes are jasmine, yuzu, orange peel and apricot. Jasmine stands out when it is hot, and the sweetness of apricot becomes clearer as it cools. When tasting, do not judge from one sip; check two or three times as it cools.</p>
<h2>Moving it onto a cafe recipe card</h2>
<p>In a cafe, add fields for grinder setting, date adjusted and person in charge to this card. The habit of checking the card again when you change coffee or open a new bag is what protects the taste. The full method is in <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/blogs/coffeelog/blog-cafe-recipe-card-sop">standardising coffee taste in your cafe</a>.</p>
<h2>Want to use this coffee in your venue</h2>
<p>We provide samples and supply terms through <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/contact?type=wholesale">wholesale enquiry</a>. Wholesale pricing is applied to your account as soon as your business is verified after <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/account/signup?role=business">business registration</a>.</p>
<h2>FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q. Which grinder is grind 700 based on?</strong><br />Every grinder has a different scale, so the number cannot be copied directly. Find the grind that finishes within 2:10 to 2:30 and write that value on your card.</p>
<p><strong>Q. Can I use this recipe for other Ethiopian washed coffees?</strong><br />As a starting point, yes. Each coffee has its own reference recipe, so check the recipe on its product page first.</p>
<p><strong>Q. Does a light roast suit milk drinks?</strong><br />We have a reference recipe based on 150ml of milk. If the acidity feels strong, using a blend for your milk menu is another option.</p>
<h3>Sources</h3>
<ul><li>Recipe and cup notes: MTSPACE COFFEE product data</li></ul>$d175$, seo_title_en = $d175$Ethiopian Washed Recipe: Kochere Danicho Filter and Espresso$d175$, seo_description_en = $d175$Reference recipe card for Ethiopia Kochere Danicho Washed: filter 15g to 225g, espresso 18g to 43g in 24 seconds, a milk recipe, and an adjustment table.$d175$ where slug = 'blog-ethiopia-washed-recipe-card' and title_en is null and body_html_en is null;
update public.content_post set title_en = $d175$Your First Wholesale Coffee Order: From Registration to Delivery and Dial-in$d175$, excerpt_en = $d175$Register as a business member and, once you are verified, wholesale pricing applies to your account straight away and you can order. Every step from sign-up to your first dial-in.$d175$, body_html_en = $d175$<p>When a cafe owner changes coffee suppliers or chooses one for the first time, the most common question is "how soon can I order after signing up?" The short answer: once you register as a business member with MTSPACE and your business is verified, wholesale pricing is applied to your account straight away, and you can order on our online store from that moment. This post walks through each step from registration to your first delivery and first dial-in.</p>
<h2>The steps at a glance</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What you do</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Consultation (optional)</td><td>Tell us your menu and guests, and request samples</td><td>Wholesale enquiry</td></tr><tr><td>2. Business registration</td><td>Enter business details and upload your business registration certificate</td><td>Sign up</td></tr><tr><td>3. Business verification</td><td>Details checked, wholesale pricing applied</td><td>MTSPACE</td></tr><tr><td>4. First order</td><td>Order wholesale-only products at wholesale prices</td><td>Online store</td></tr><tr><td>5. Delivery and dial-in</td><td>Start from the reference recipe and fit it to your equipment</td><td>Your cafe</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>1. Consultation is optional, but recommended</h2>
<p>If you have not decided which coffee suits your cafe, send a <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/contact?type=wholesale">wholesale enquiry</a> with your menu, daily cup count and number of grinders. We will share samples and supply terms. Cafes starting small can also receive supply.</p>
<h2>2. Business registration</h2>
<p>On the <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/account/signup?role=business">business registration</a> page, enter your business details and upload your business registration certificate. Korean tax invoices are issued to approved business members, based on the business details registered in My Account.</p>
<h2>3. Wholesale pricing applies as soon as you are verified</h2>
<p>Once MTSPACE checks your details, the wholesale catalogue and your member pricing are applied to your account straight away. Before verification, you can only order regular products at retail prices. You can see your approval status in My Account.</p>
<h2>4. Base your first order on your menu</h2>
<p>The wholesale line has seven coffees: Spotlight, Allrounder, Classic Blend, EZPZ, Yirgacheffe Decaf, Damn Good and House Blend. Choose your first order to fit your menu.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Your situation</th><th>Starting point</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>One coffee for both black and lattes</td><td>Allrounder or Classic Blend</td></tr><tr><td>Mostly rich, full-bodied lattes</td><td>Damn Good or House Blend</td></tr><tr><td>A bright black menu</td><td>Spotlight</td></tr><tr><td>Lower caffeine for afternoon guests</td><td>EZPZ (low caffeine blend)</td></tr><tr><td>A decaf menu</td><td>Yirgacheffe Decaf</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>All our blends use Ethiopian green coffee as their base. You can compare the blends in <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/blogs/coffeelog/blog-blend-4-comparison">our four blends compared</a>. For order quantities, see <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/blogs/coffeelog/blog-wholesale-order-quantity">how to calculate your cafe's coffee order</a>.</p>
<h2>5. Delivery and first dial-in</h2>
<p>Within Korea, delivery takes 1 to 2 days after dispatch (Lotte Global Logistics), and remote and island areas may take an extra 1 to 2 days. We do not roast ahead and stockpile beans; we roast on a set cycle to match orders and then ship.</p>
<p>When the coffee arrives, start from the reference recipe on its product page. For example, the Allrounder espresso reference is 20g in, 40g out, in 25 seconds. Write the values you adjusted for your machine and grinder back onto your recipe card. How to set one up is in <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/blogs/coffeelog/blog-cafe-recipe-card-sop">standardising coffee taste in your cafe</a>.</p>
<h2>FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q. Can I order at wholesale prices as soon as I sign up?</strong><br />Wholesale pricing is applied to your account as soon as your business registration certificate has been checked. Before that, orders are at retail prices only.</p>
<p><strong>Q. Can I order regularly?</strong><br />Yes, set a cycle that matches your cafe's usage and order on it. We can agree order quantity and frequency with you in a consultation.</p>
<p><strong>Q. How much is shipping for large orders?</strong><br />A standard shipping fee applies to orders under 10 kg, and a separate policy applies to bulk orders of 10 kg or more. You can see the exact amount at checkout.</p>
<h3>Sources</h3>
<ul><li>Registration, business verification, wholesale pricing, tax invoices and shipping: MTSPACE COFFEE FAQ and member policy</li><li>Wholesale line and reference recipes: MTSPACE COFFEE product data</li></ul>$d175$, seo_title_en = $d175$First Wholesale Coffee Order Guide: Registration and Pricing$d175$, seo_description_en = $d175$How to place your first MTSPACE wholesale order: business registration, wholesale pricing applied on verification, choosing coffees by menu, delivery and first dial-in.$d175$ where slug = 'blog-wholesale-first-order-guide' and title_en is null and body_html_en is null;
update public.content_post set title_en = $d175$How Much Coffee Should Your Cafe Order: Weekly Usage From Daily Cup Sales$d175$, excerpt_en = $d175$Calculate your coffee order from daily cup sales, not from feel. A weekly usage formula, a worked example, and how to set your order cycle and reorder point.$d175$, body_html_en = $d175$<p>Many cafes run short of coffee and have to order in a hurry mid-service, or end up with too much stock and use beans that have lost their aroma. The short answer: calculate your order from daily cup sales, not from feel. Work out weekly usage as "cups per day × dose per cup × trading days × loss allowance", then fix your order cycle around that amount.</p>
<h2>The basic formula</h2>
<blockquote><p>Weekly coffee usage (g) = cups sold per day × dose per cup (g) × trading days per week × (1 + loss allowance)</p></blockquote>
<p>The loss allowance covers shots discarded while dialling in, tasting and accidental waste. If you have no records yet, start at 10% and adjust after a month of notes.</p>
<h2>Worked example</h2>
<p>The figures below are <strong>assumed numbers</strong> to show the method. Assume a cafe sells 100 cups a day, trades six days a week and uses Allrounder. Allrounder is a medium roast blend of Ethiopian natural and washed green coffee, with a reference recipe of 20g for espresso and 22g for milk drinks.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Menu</th><th>Cups per day (assumed)</th><th>Dose</th><th>Daily usage</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Americano</td><td>40 cups</td><td>20g</td><td>800g</td></tr><tr><td>Milk drinks</td><td>60 cups</td><td>22g</td><td>1,320g</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>100 cups</td><td></td><td>2,120g</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Weekly usage is 2,120g × 6 days × 1.1 = 13,992g, about 14 kg. This cafe should plan its orders around roughly 14 kg a week.</p>
<h2>Fix your order cycle</h2>
<p>Buying a lot at once lowers shipping per unit, but the coffee sits longer. Ordering too often means more work placing orders and checking deliveries. For most cafes the simplest approach is to fix the cycle around weekly usage and only change the amount when sales rise or fall.</p>
<p>We do not roast ahead and stockpile beans; coffee is roasted to match orders and then shipped, arriving within 1 to 2 days of dispatch within Korea. Set a reorder point so you order while you still have enough to last until the next delivery.</p>
<blockquote><p>Reorder point = daily usage × (days from ordering to delivery + one spare day)</p></blockquote>
<h2>What to check when stock is left over</h2>
<ul><li>Whether cup sales have dropped, or it is a seasonal effect</li><li>Whether your dose has drifted below the recipe</li><li>Whether several opened bags are in use at the same time</li></ul>
<p>Roasted coffee loses aroma when it meets air, light, heat and moisture. For storage, see <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/blogs/coffeelog/blog-coffee-bean-storage">how to store coffee beans</a>.</p>
<h2>Want help setting your order quantity</h2>
<p>Tell us your daily cup sales and menu, and we will work out your coffee line-up and order cycle with you. Please send a <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/contact?type=wholesale">wholesale enquiry</a>. Cafes starting small can also receive supply. Wholesale pricing is applied to your account as soon as your business is verified after <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/account/signup?role=business">business registration</a>. The process is in <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/blogs/coffeelog/blog-wholesale-first-order-guide">your first wholesale coffee order</a>.</p>
<h2>FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q. What if I do not know my daily cup sales?</strong><br />Pull only the coffee menu cup counts from a week of POS sales and take the daily average. Splitting by menu item makes it more accurate.</p>
<p><strong>Q. Is a 10% loss allowance too much?</strong><br />It is only a starting point. Compare actual usage with the calculated figure over a month and raise or lower it to fit your cafe.</p>
<p><strong>Q. What if I use two or more coffees?</strong><br />Run the same formula for each coffee separately and add them up. A slow seller such as decaf can be ordered on a longer cycle.</p>
<h3>Sources</h3>
<ul><li>Dose by menu: MTSPACE COFFEE Allrounder reference recipe (product data)</li><li>Delivery time and roasting approach: MTSPACE COFFEE FAQ</li><li>Cup counts in the table are assumed numbers for the worked example</li></ul>$d175$, seo_title_en = $d175$Cafe Coffee Order Quantity: The Weekly Usage Formula$d175$, seo_description_en = $d175$Calculate your cafe coffee order from daily cups, dose, trading days and a loss allowance. A worked example, fixed order cycles, reorder points and what to check when stock is left over.$d175$ where slug = 'blog-wholesale-order-quantity' and title_en is null and body_html_en is null;
update public.content_post set title_en = $d175$Compare Wholesale Coffee by Cost per Cup: Turning Price per Kilogram Into Cost per Cup$d175$, excerpt_en = $d175$Compare coffee prices by cost per cup, not price per kilogram. The calculation, how dose differs by menu, and three costs a price list does not show.$d175$, body_html_en = $d175$<p>When comparing coffee suppliers, many cafes simply line up the price per kilogram on each price list. But the number that actually reaches your margin is not the price per kilogram; it is the coffee cost in each cup. The short answer: convert prices with "cost per cup = price per kg ÷ 1,000 × dose per cup", then look at wasted coffee and taste consistency as well, and the real difference becomes visible.</p>
<h2>How to calculate cost per cup</h2>
<p>The calculation is simple. Divide the price per kilogram by 1,000 to get the price per gram, then multiply by the coffee weight used in one cup.</p>
<blockquote><p>Coffee cost per cup = price per kg ÷ 1,000 × dose per cup (g)</p></blockquote>
<p>The figures below are <strong>assumed numbers</strong> to show the method. They have nothing to do with actual wholesale prices.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Assumed</th><th>Price per kg</th><th>Price per gram</th><th>18g dose</th><th>22g dose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Coffee A</td><td>₩30,000</td><td>₩30</td><td>₩540</td><td>₩660</td></tr><tr><td>Coffee B</td><td>₩33,000</td><td>₩33</td><td>₩594</td><td>₩726</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>A ₩3,000 difference per kilogram becomes a ₩54 to ₩66 difference per cup. A gap that looks large on a price list is smaller than you might think at the level of a single cup.</p>
<h2>When the dose changes, so does the cost</h2>
<p>Even with the same coffee, cost per cup changes with the dose for each menu item. Taking the reference recipe for MTSPACE Allrounder as an example, espresso uses 20g, milk drinks 22g and pour over 12g. A cafe that sells mostly lattes should calculate on the milk dose to stay close to reality.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Menu (Allrounder)</th><th>Dose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Espresso</td><td>20g</td></tr><tr><td>Milk drinks</td><td>22g</td></tr><tr><td>Pour over</td><td>12g</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>Three costs a price list does not show</h2>
<p><strong>Wasted coffee.</strong> The shots you discard while dialling in each morning, and when you open a new bag, are coffee too. A coffee that shifts in taste from lot to lot means more dial-ins and a higher real cost.</p>
<p><strong>Inconsistent taste.</strong> If the taste changes with every delivery, you have to change your recipe often, and the variation guests notice affects whether they come back. Even with a slightly lower price, this cost can be bigger.</p>
<p><strong>Stock loss.</strong> Ordering too much and then using or throwing away coffee that has lost its aroma is a cost as well. A supplier that lets you plan your order cycle reduces stock loss.</p>
<h2>Questions to ask when comparing</h2>
<ul><li>Do they roast ahead and stockpile, or roast to match orders?</li><li>Does each delivered lot taste the same?</li><li>Do they give you a reference recipe for each menu item?</li><li>Can you start small?</li></ul>
<p>MTSPACE does not roast ahead and stockpile beans; we roast on a set cycle to match orders. All our blends use Ethiopian green coffee as their base, and we blend green coffees of similar bean size so the top and bottom of a bag taste the same. The detailed criteria are in <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/blogs/coffeelog/blog-wholesale-bean-freshness">how to choose a wholesale coffee</a>.</p>
<h2>Check actual wholesale prices</h2>
<p>MTSPACE wholesale pricing is applied to your account as soon as your business is verified after <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/account/signup?role=business">business registration</a>, and you can then see it on each product page. If you would like to work out cost per cup for your menu with us, please send a <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/contact?type=wholesale">wholesale enquiry</a>. Supply terms and the process are in our <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/wholesale">cafe coffee supply guide</a>.</p>
<h2>FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q. Is there a public price list?</strong><br />Wholesale prices are tiered rates applied to your account after business verification. You can see them on product pages after you register.</p>
<p><strong>Q. Why are milk and cups left out of cost per cup?</strong><br />This post is about comparing coffee suppliers, so it covers only the coffee cost. Calculate your full menu cost separately, adding milk, cups and syrups.</p>
<p><strong>Q. Doesn't a smaller dose lower the cost?</strong><br />It does, but the coffee gets weaker. It is better to compare cost within the range where you keep to the recipe.</p>
<h3>Sources</h3>
<ul><li>Dose by menu: MTSPACE COFFEE Allrounder reference recipe (product data)</li><li>Roasting and blending standards and how wholesale pricing applies: MTSPACE COFFEE FAQ and supply guide</li><li>Prices in the table are assumed numbers for the worked example</li></ul>$d175$, seo_title_en = $d175$Comparing Wholesale Coffee Prices: Cost per Cup$d175$, seo_description_en = $d175$Compare cafe wholesale coffee by cost per cup instead of price per kilogram. The formula, dose by menu, wasted coffee, taste consistency and stock loss.$d175$ where slug = 'blog-wholesale-price-per-cup' and title_en is null and body_html_en is null;
update public.content_post set title_en = $d175$Yirgacheffe vs Kochere: Telling an Origin Name From a District Name$d175$, excerpt_en = $d175$Yirgacheffe is a broad origin name, and Kochere is a smaller district inside it. How the place names fit together, why the taste differs, and how Kochere Danicho compares with Yirgacheffe Decaf.$d175$, body_html_en = $d175$<p>Put a Yirgacheffe coffee next to a Kochere coffee and it is easy to wonder whether they are the same coffee or different ones. The short answer: Yirgacheffe is a broad origin name, and Kochere is a smaller district inside it. Kochere coffee is part of Yirgacheffe coffee, but the name Yirgacheffe alone does not tell you which district a coffee came from or whose hands it passed through.</p>
<h2>Yirgacheffe and Kochere: the place names first</h2>
<p>Coffee grown in the Gedeo zone of southern Ethiopia is commonly traded on the international market under the name Yirgacheffe. Kochere is one of the woredas (administrative districts) within the Gedeo zone. So a label that reads Yirgacheffe Kochere is using the broad origin name and the narrower district name together.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Name</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Country</td><td>Ethiopia</td><td>Where the coffee comes from</td></tr><tr><td>Origin</td><td>Yirgacheffe (Gedeo)</td><td>The broad origin name used on the international market</td></tr><tr><td>District</td><td>Kochere</td><td>A woreda within the Gedeo zone</td></tr><tr><td>Village, producer</td><td>Hama, Danicho Mukere</td><td>The washing station that collects cherries, and its producer</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h2>Why coffees named Yirgacheffe taste different</h2>
<p>There are three reasons cups differ under the same Yirgacheffe name. The first is altitude. The higher the farm, the bigger the gap between day and night temperatures, so cherries ripen slowly and the green coffee becomes denser. The second is processing. Even cherries from the same district lean clear and vivid when washed, and toward rich fruit sweetness when natural. The third is the mix of varieties. Ethiopian landraces and research institute selections grow together across this area, and the proportions differ from farm to farm.</p>
<p>That is why a coffee labelled with its district, producer and process is easier to predict than one labelled with an origin name alone.</p>
<h2>What does Kochere Danicho Washed taste like</h2>
<p>MTSPACE <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/products/ethiopia-kochere-danicho">Ethiopia Kochere Danicho Washed</a> comes from the washing station run by producer Danicho Mukere in Kochere Hama. It is a washed lot of Ethiopian landraces and 74110, 74112 and 74158, grown at 1,980 to 2,250 metres above sea level.</p>
<p>The cup notes are jasmine, yuzu, orange peel and apricot. White floral aroma comes first while it is hot, and the sweetness of apricot rises as it cools. As a single origin, it is light roasted.</p>
<h2>How is it different from Yirgacheffe Decaf</h2>
<p>The MTSPACE line has one more coffee carrying the Yirgacheffe name: the wholesale-only <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/products/yirga-decaf-1000">Ethiopia Yirgacheffe Decaf</a>.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Kochere Danicho Washed</th><th>Yirgacheffe Decaf</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Origin on the label</td><td>Yirgacheffe, Kochere, Hama</td><td>Ethiopia Yirgacheffe</td></tr><tr><td>Process</td><td>Washed</td><td>C2 decaffeination</td></tr><tr><td>Roast</td><td>Light</td><td>Medium</td></tr><tr><td>Cup notes</td><td>Jasmine, yuzu, orange peel, apricot</td><td>Herb, citrus, cacao</td></tr><tr><td>Use</td><td>A filter cup for enjoying aroma</td><td>A cafe decaf menu</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>The two coffees share an origin name but serve different purposes. Kochere shows the floral aroma and acidity of its origin, while the decaf focuses on reducing caffeine while keeping a Yirgacheffe-style brightness.</p>
<h2>Want to use this coffee in your venue</h2>
<p>We provide supply terms and samples through <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/contact?type=wholesale">wholesale enquiry</a>. Wholesale pricing is applied to your account as soon as your business is verified after <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/account/signup?role=business">business registration</a>. The process is in our <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/wholesale">cafe coffee supply guide</a>.</p>
<h2>FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q. Which is the better coffee, Yirgacheffe or Kochere?</strong><br />It is not a difference in quality but in the scope of the label. Kochere is a district within Yirgacheffe, so it is a name that gives narrower information.</p>
<p><strong>Q. Should I avoid coffees labelled only Yirgacheffe?</strong><br />No. But if you want to predict the taste, it helps to check the district, producer and process as well.</p>
<p><strong>Q. How should I brew Kochere Danicho?</strong><br />Start with 15g for filter, grind 700, a 45g bloom for 90 seconds, 225g total, finishing in 2:10 to 2:30. Detailed adjustments are in the <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/blogs/coffeelog/blog-ethiopia-washed-recipe-card">Ethiopian washed recipe card</a>.</p>
<h3>Sources</h3>
<ul><li>Producer, area, altitude, varieties and process: MTSPACE COFFEE product data and green coffee importer lot information</li><li>Yirgacheffe (Gedeo) and the Kochere woreda: <a href="https://www.trabocca.com/our-coffees/ethiopia/yirgacheffe/">Trabocca, Yirgacheffe region (Gedeo)</a></li><li>Decaffeination process and cup notes: MTSPACE COFFEE product data</li></ul>$d175$, seo_title_en = $d175$Yirgacheffe vs Kochere: Ethiopian Origin Names Explained$d175$, seo_description_en = $d175$Yirgacheffe is the broad origin name and Kochere a district within it. The place name structure, why cups differ, and Kochere Danicho Washed compared with Yirgacheffe Decaf.$d175$ where slug = 'blog-yirgacheffe-vs-kochere' and title_en is null and body_html_en is null;
update public.content_post set title_en = $d175$Baja Verapaz Microlot: Caramel, Milk Chocolate and a Family-Farm Guatemala Washed$d175$, excerpt_en = $d175$Catuai and Caturra grown by family farms in Cubulco, Baja Verapaz, Guatemala and washed on each farm. A light roast single origin that opens lightly with orange and finishes round with caramel and milk chocolate.$d175$, body_html_en = $d175$<p>If sweetness and balance matter more to you than acidity in your everyday coffee, the Baja Verapaz Microlot is an easy one to reach for. It is a lot of Catuai and Caturra grown by family farms in Cubulco in central Guatemala and washed on each farm, and MTSPACE has roasted it light. The short answer: it opens lightly with orange and finishes round with caramel and milk chocolate.</p>
<p><a href="https://mtspace.coffee/products/guatemala-baja-verapaz-microlot"><strong>See Guatemala Baja Verapaz Microlot Washed</strong> →</a></p>
<h2>What is Baja Verapaz like?</h2>
<p>Baja Verapaz is a coffee region in central Guatemala. It is still a less familiar name than Antigua or Huehuetenango, but in this area of pine forests and valleys, smallholders grow coffee together with their families. This lot was produced in Cubulco.</p>
<p>The average farm is about 0.5 hectares. Many families farm land handed down to them and draw on long growing experience to raise quality and productivity. Income from coffee goes to the family's living and to caring for the farm for the next harvest. The varieties are Catuai and Caturra, grown at 1,500 to 1,800 metres above sea level.</p>
<h2>Hand-picked and washed on the farm</h2>
<p>At harvest, ripe cherries are selected and picked by hand. Processing is washed, done by each farm itself. The fruit is removed, the coffee is fermented and washed, then dried in the sun. Farmers manage every step by hand, right up to tidying the dried parchment.</p>
<p>This lot is a microlot that the green coffee importer brought in as a single origin, kept separate from its usual blending selections.</p>
<h2>It opens with orange and closes with almond</h2>
<p>The cup notes are <strong>orange, red apple, caramel, milk chocolate and roasted almond</strong>.</p>
<p>The first sip brings a light acidity of orange and red apple. The acidity does not linger, and the sweetness of caramel and milk chocolate soon fills the cup. The finish leaves the nuttiness of roasted almond and a round texture.</p>
<p>If you have put off single origins because acidity feels like too much, start with this one. Its centre is the sweetness and balance of a Guatemalan washed coffee, so it is easy to drink morning or afternoon.</p>
<p><a href="https://mtspace.coffee/products/guatemala-baja-verapaz-microlot"><strong>Taste the sweetness and balance for yourself, ₩15,000</strong> →</a></p>
<h2>Light roasting is not under-roasting</h2>
<p>Light roasts that leave only acidity and miss sweetness are common. That is why MTSPACE applied the same light profile we use in competition to this lot.</p>
<blockquote><p>First crack at 6 minutes 30 seconds, a 5℃ rise after crack, 45 seconds of development</p></blockquote>
<p>The setting raises the temperature only 5℃ after crack and stops at 45 seconds. The aim is to secure enough time after crack for caramel and milk chocolate sweetness to develop, even in a light roast.</p>
<h2>Who it suits</h2>
<ul><li>Home brewers who look for sweetness and balance before acidity</li><li>Anyone moving from blends to a single origin for the first time</li><li>Cafes looking for an approachable single origin for an everyday filter menu</li></ul>
<p>If you would like to start with a blend, see <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/collections/blends">all blends</a>; to see other single origins, see <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/collections/single-origins">all single origins</a>.</p>
<h2>Recommended recipe</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Brew</th><th>Dose</th><th>Yield</th><th>Time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Filter</td><td>15g, grind 700</td><td>45g bloom, 90 seconds / 225g total</td><td>2:10 to 2:30</td></tr><tr><td>Espresso</td><td>18g</td><td>43g</td><td>24 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>Milk</td><td>20g</td><td>40g (150ml milk)</td><td>26 seconds</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>This is the MTSPACE reference recipe for washed coffees. Start with these numbers and adjust the grind as you taste. It suits both filter and espresso; at home, we suggest starting with filter.</p>
<h2>Specs</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Details</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Origin</td><td>Cubulco, Baja Verapaz, Guatemala</td></tr><tr><td>Producers</td><td>Smallholders of the Cubulco area</td></tr><tr><td>Altitude</td><td>1,500 to 1,800m</td></tr><tr><td>Varieties</td><td>Catuai, Caturra</td></tr><tr><td>Process</td><td>Washed</td></tr><tr><td>Roast</td><td>Light</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p><a href="https://mtspace.coffee/products/guatemala-baja-verapaz-microlot"><strong>Add to cart, ₩15,000</strong> →</a></p>
<h2>For businesses using it on your menu</h2>
<p>To run a single origin as a filter or seasonal menu in your venue, please register as a business member. After your business details are checked and approved, you can order at your tier's wholesale price. Tell us your menu and grinder setup first, and we will also help with samples and recipes.</p>
<ul><li><a href="https://mtspace.coffee/contact">Send a wholesale enquiry</a></li><li><a href="https://mtspace.coffee/account/signup">Register as a business</a></li></ul>
<h2>FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q. It is a light roast. Is it very sour?</strong><br />A light acidity of orange and red apple opens the cup and soon moves into the sweetness of caramel and milk chocolate. Acidity is not the centre of this coffee.</p>
<p><strong>Q. What does microlot mean?</strong><br />It means a lot managed and sold separately from an individual farm or a small plot. This coffee is a washed lot grown by smallholders in Cubulco.</p>
<p><strong>Q. Can I use it on a cafe menu?</strong><br />Its sweetness and balance make it a good everyday filter coffee. Register as a business member and, once approved, you can order at your tier's wholesale price.</p>
<h3>Sources</h3>
<ul><li>Producers, area, altitude, varieties, farm size, process and cup notes: green coffee importer lot information</li><li>Roast profile: MTSPACE COFFEE roasting records</li></ul>$d175$, seo_title_en = $d175$Guatemala Baja Verapaz Microlot Washed: Orange, Caramel, Milk Chocolate$d175$, seo_description_en = $d175$A washed microlot of Catuai and Caturra grown at 1,500 to 1,800m by smallholders in Cubulco, Baja Verapaz. A light roast single origin of orange, red apple, caramel, milk chocolate and roasted almond.$d175$ where slug = 'blog-guatemala-baja-verapaz-microlot' and title_en is null and body_html_en is null;
update public.content_post set title_en = $d175$Feleke Jemberu Natural: Lavender, Yellow Peach and the Late-Ripening Cherries of Guji Uraga$d175$, excerpt_en = $d175$Cherries from about 250 smallholder families at 2,000 to 2,200m, delivered to the Feleke Jemberu washing station in Uraga, Guji and dried as a natural. A light roast single origin with yellow peach and apricot jam sweetness beneath lavender.$d175$, body_html_en = $d175$<p>If you want to take your time over a coffee full of fruit aroma at home on a weekend afternoon, Feleke Jemberu Natural is a good fit. It is a lot of cherries grown by about 250 highland smallholder families in Uraga, Guji, Ethiopia and dried as a natural, and MTSPACE has roasted it light. The short answer: the sweetness of yellow peach and apricot jam settles with weight beneath an aroma of lavender.</p>
<p><a href="https://mtspace.coffee/products/ethiopia-uraga-feleke-jemberu-natural"><strong>See Ethiopia Guji Uraga Feleke Jemberu Natural</strong> →</a></p>
<h2>Uraga's late-ripening cherries</h2>
<p>Uraga is a highland coffee area in Guji, Ethiopia. Its farms sit at 2,000 to 2,200 metres above sea level, and relatively cool temperatures make the harvest late. Cherry picking usually runs from late November to mid-January.</p>
<p>The Feleke Jemberu washing station was founded in 2010. Today about 250 smallholder families deliver cherries to it, each farming about 1.75 hectares on average. The station processes about 800,000 kg of cherries a year.</p>
<h2>Picking only ripe cherries, drying for 12 to 15 days</h2>
<p>At harvest, the emphasis is on picking only ripe cherries. Rather than stripping a whole branch at once, pickers take the ripe cherries first and leave unripe ones on the tree to pick again later. The station and its export partner train producers in this way of harvesting and in farm management.</p>
<p>The process is natural. Selected cherries are spread on drying beds for about 12 to 15 days and turned often so moisture leaves evenly. Drying ends when moisture reaches about 11 to 12%. The station records every step, from cherry intake through drying and storage to export preparation, with receipts and lot labels.</p>
<h2>Yellow peach and apricot jam over lavender</h2>
<p>The cup notes are <strong>lavender, orange, yellow peach, apricot jam and sugarcane</strong>.</p>
<p>On the nose, a purple floral aroma like lavender comes first. The sip opens with the bright acidity of orange, then the rich sweetness of yellow peach and apricot jam adds weight. The finish leaves a clean trace of sugarcane.</p>
<p>Natural processing dries the coffee inside the whole cherry, so more fruit sweetness and texture remain than in a washed coffee. As a result, the jam-like sweetness becomes clearer as the cup cools.</p>
<p><a href="https://mtspace.coffee/products/ethiopia-uraga-feleke-jemberu-natural"><strong>Taste the lavender and yellow peach for yourself, ₩19,000</strong> →</a></p>
<h2>Light roasting is not under-roasting</h2>
<p>Light roasts that leave only acidity and miss sweetness are common. That is why MTSPACE applied the same light profile we use in competition to this lot.</p>
<blockquote><p>First crack at 6 minutes 30 seconds, a 5℃ rise after crack, 45 seconds of development</p></blockquote>
<p>The setting raises the temperature only 5℃ after crack and stops at 45 seconds. The aim is to stop before the natural's fruitiness turns heavy, keeping the lavender aroma and orange acidity clear.</p>
<h2>Who it suits</h2>
<ul><li>Home brewers who love fruit-forward Ethiopian naturals</li><li>Anyone who wants to compare washed and natural from the same origin</li><li>Cafes looking for a filter coffee to serve with desserts</li></ul>
