update public.content_post set title_en = $d175$Santa Rosa Pacamara Honey: Lot 244 From a Cup of Excellence Winning Farm$d175$, excerpt_en = $d175$Finca Santa Rosa in Chalatenango, El Salvador has won first place at the Cup of Excellence several times with Pacamara Honey. Its lot 244 is a light roast single origin where a syrupy sweetness runs long beneath apple, peach and apricot.$d175$, body_html_en = $d175$<p>If you are looking for a cup to brew slowly on a special day, Santa Rosa Pacamara Honey fits that moment. Finca Santa Rosa in Chalatenango, El Salvador has won first place at the Cup of Excellence several times with Pacamara Honey, and this coffee is lot 244 from that farm. The short answer: a syrupy sweetness like sugarcane runs long beneath the fruit of apple, peach and apricot.</p>
<p><a href="https://mtspace.coffee/products/el-salvador-santa-rosa-pacamara-honey"><strong>See El Salvador Santa Rosa Pacamara Honey</strong> →</a></p>
<h2>Pacamara beneath a pine forest</h2>
<p>Santa Rosa is a farm in San Ignacio, Chalatenango, in northern El Salvador. The Rivera family's coffee farming began in 1979, when the father, Jorge Raúl Rivera, started at La Palma. Today his son, Jorge Raúl Rivera Jr., carries on the farm.</p>
<p>The father first planned to turn Santa Rosa into a coffee farm with his brother José Roberto Rivera, but a coffee price crisis changed the plan and they planted pine trees instead. The turning point came in 2003, around the time the Cup of Excellence arrived in El Salvador. Pacamara was planted on this land then, and the pine forest that had grown first became shade for the coffee trees. The farm sits at 1,700 metres above sea level.</p>
<h2>Pacamara, honey processing and a competition record</h2>
<p>Pacamara is a variety bred by El Salvador's coffee research institute by crossing Pacas and Maragogype. Its large beans and high flavour potential have made it a signature variety of El Salvador.</p>
<p>Santa Rosa took first place at the Cup of Excellence with Pacamara Honey in 2014, 2017 and 2019. Its recent results are first place in the washed and honey category in 2025 and fifth place in the same category in 2026. They reflect the father's growing knowledge combined with the son's processing research.</p>
<p>The farm processes its Pacamara four ways: washed, honey, anaerobic honey and natural. This coffee is the honey.</p>
<h2>Fruit, florals and a long syrupy sweetness</h2>
<p>The cup notes are <strong>floral, peach, apple, apricot and sugarcane syrup</strong>.</p>
<p>As you lift the cup, a floral aroma spreads first. The sip opens with apple, peach and apricot in turn, and a sweetness like sugarcane syrup carries on long behind them.</p>
<p>Honey processing removes the fruit and dries the coffee with its mucilage left on, so it sits between the cleanness of a washed coffee and the sweetness of a natural. The flavour of Pacamara's large beans meets this sweetness for a fuller texture.</p>
<p><a href="https://mtspace.coffee/products/el-salvador-santa-rosa-pacamara-honey"><strong>Taste the honey from a competition farm for yourself, ₩22,000</strong> →</a></p>
<h2>Light roasting is not under-roasting</h2>
<p>Light roasts that leave only acidity and miss sweetness are common. That is why MTSPACE applied the same light profile we use in competition to this lot.</p>
<blockquote><p>First crack at 6 minutes 30 seconds, a 5℃ rise after crack, 45 seconds of development</p></blockquote>
<p>The setting raises the temperature only 5℃ after crack and stops at 45 seconds. The aim is to protect the sweetness of the honey process while keeping the floral aroma and fruit acidity together.</p>
<h2>Who it suits</h2>
<ul><li>Home brewers who want to taste coffee from a competition-proven farm</li><li>Anyone trying the Pacamara variety for the first time</li><li>Cafes preparing a limited seasonal single origin menu</li></ul>
