<ul><li>Farm, owner, area, altitude, variety, process, competition record and cup notes: green coffee importer lot information</li><li>Roast profile: MTSPACE COFFEE roasting records</li></ul>$d175$, seo_title_en = $d175$El Salvador Santa Rosa Pacamara Honey: Peach, Apricot, Sugarcane Syrup$d175$, seo_description_en = $d175$Pacamara Honey lot 244 from Finca Santa Rosa, Chalatenango, El Salvador, a multiple Cup of Excellence winner. 1,700m, a light roast of floral, peach, apple, apricot and sugarcane syrup, with the farm's story.$d175$ where slug = 'blog-el-salvador-santa-rosa-pacamara-honey' and title_en is null and body_html_en is null;
update public.content_post set title_en = $d175$Honduras Montecillos Washed: Black Cherry, Molasses and the Round Sweetness of Bourbon$d175$, excerpt_en = $d175$Bourbon grown at 1,650m on Finca Montecillos in Pozo Negro, Intibucá, Honduras by Wilmer Alexis Grau, seventh at the 2005 Cup of Excellence, and washed. A light roast single origin with plum jam and molasses sweetness beneath black cherry and red grape.$d175$, body_html_en = $d175$<p>If you want deep fruit sweetness together with a round texture that is not heavy, we recommend Honduras Montecillos Washed. It is a washed lot of Bourbon grown by producer Wilmer Alexis Grau on Finca Montecillos in Intibucá, and MTSPACE has roasted it light. The short answer: the sweetness of plum jam and molasses settles beneath the fruit of black cherry and red grape.</p>
<p><a href="https://mtspace.coffee/products/honduras-montecillos-washed"><strong>See Honduras Montecillos Washed</strong> →</a></p>
<h2>Honduras, from blending coffee to microlots</h2>
<p>Honduras produces and exports more coffee than any other country in Central America. Mountains above 1,000 metres run across the country, and climate and soil differ clearly from region to region. Plentiful rainfall and wide swings between day and night temperatures let cherries ripen slowly and build up sweetness.</p>
<p>Honduran coffee was once known mainly as mild, easy blending coffee. Today, research and training by the coffee institute IHCAFE and producers' steady quality improvements have greatly advanced production and processing. As more microlots are traced to individual farms and producers, each area's character is showing more clearly.</p>
<h2>Montecillos in Pozo Negro, a farm inherited from a father</h2>
<p>Montecillos is a farm in the village of Pozo Negro, Masaguara, Intibucá. The area belongs to the Montecillos region in Honduras's coffee region classification. Producer Wilmer Alexis Grau learned coffee farming from his father as a child and took over the family farm after his father passed away. His father, José Javier Grau Gómez, began farming in 1980 on less than half a hectare.</p>
<p>The farm covers about 4 hectares and grows Bourbon at 1,650 metres above sea level. At harvest, 40 to 50 pickers select and pick only ripe cherries by hand. The process is washed. Grau placed seventh at the 2005 Honduras Cup of Excellence, and in 2017 he entered under the Montecillos farm name and scored 86.22.</p>
<h2>It opens with black cherry and fills with molasses</h2>
<p>The cup notes are <strong>orange marmalade, black cherry, red grape, plum jam and molasses</strong>.</p>
<p>The first sip brings a gentle acidity of orange marmalade. Then the deep fruit of black cherry and red grape opens, and the sweetness of plum jam and molasses adds weight. It finishes softly with a round texture.</p>
<p>It has a different character from the bright acidity of an Ethiopian. Dark red fruit and deep sweetness are at its centre, so it suits anyone who looks for sweetness and texture before acidity.</p>
<p><a href="https://mtspace.coffee/products/honduras-montecillos-washed"><strong>Taste the dark red fruit sweetness for yourself, ₩18,000</strong> →</a></p>
<h2>Light roasting is not under-roasting</h2>
<p>Light roasts that leave only acidity and miss sweetness are common. That is why MTSPACE applied the same light profile we use in competition to this lot.</p>
<blockquote><p>First crack at 6 minutes 30 seconds, a 5℃ rise after crack, 45 seconds of development</p></blockquote>
<p>The setting raises the temperature only 5℃ after crack and stops at 45 seconds. The aim is to protect Bourbon's sweetness and round texture while keeping the black cherry and red grape fruit from turning heavy.</p>
<h2>Who it suits</h2>
<ul><li>Home brewers who love dark red fruit and deep sweetness</li><li>Anyone looking for a single origin with a different character from Ethiopia</li><li>Cafes looking for a single origin that moves between filter and espresso</li></ul>
<p>If you would like to start with a blend, see <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/collections/blends">all blends</a>; to see other single origins, see <a href="https://mtspace.coffee/collections/single-origins">all single origins</a>.</p>
<h2>Recommended recipe</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Brew</th><th>Dose</th><th>Yield</th><th>Time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Filter</td><td>15g, grind 700</td><td>45g bloom, 90 seconds / 225g total</td><td>2:10 to 2:30</td></tr><tr><td>Espresso</td><td>18g</td><td>43g</td><td>24 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>Milk</td><td>20g</td><td>40g (150ml milk)</td><td>26 seconds</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>This is the MTSPACE reference recipe for washed coffees. Start with these numbers and adjust the grind as you taste. To see the balance of fruit and sweetness, we suggest starting with filter.</p>
